## VI. The Enclosures

**The lineage.** First enclosure: land — hedges and Acts of Parliament, breaking the commons the Sovereign Citizen stood on. Second enclosure (Boyle's term): the intangible commons of the mind — patents on genes, copyright expansion, the fencing of texts. Third, now underway: the enclosure of *craft* — tacit knowledge itself.

**Physical AI as the third enclosure.** Tacit knowledge (Michael Polanyi: we know more than we can tell) was historically the one form of capital that could never be fully enclosed, because it lived in bodies and walked out the door every evening. The noncompete was the crude instrument for fencing the container — the worker — while the knowledge stayed inside her, mobile and negotiable. Physical AI dissolves the problem the noncompete was invented to manage: extract the process knowledge into model weights and the worker's exit no longer takes anything with it. A noncompete enforced against knowledge itself rather than against persons — permanent, universal, requiring no court. The program is old (Ure celebrating the self-acting mule commissioned to break the spinners, 1835; Taylor's transfer of craft from the worker's head to management's filing cabinet; Braverman's separation of conception from execution); physical AI is its terminal form — Marx's general intellect fragment with a $41B valuation.

**The Prometheus case study (verified June 2026).** Bezos/Bajaj's Prometheus: $12B Series B at ~$41B valuation; investors include JPMorgan, BlackRock, Goldman Sachs — incumbent balance sheets purchasing the disruption before it occurs. Building an "artificial general engineer." The confession at the center: there is no "Internet of manufacturing data" to ingest — the knowledge was never written down where it could be scraped. Hence the reported ~$100B affiliated fund to buy legacy industrial companies whose operations feed data into the model: enclosure in the strict historical sense, a commons of distributed embodied know-how converted into private property in a single vault. Analysts describe the structure as "less a portfolio than a supply chain." The loop closes: fund owns factories → factories generate data → data trains model → model redesigns factories — no point on the circle exposed to outside entry. Investors' own selling point: physical AI is "defensible" because the physical world creates moats code cannot — the venture word for technology engineered to be non-disruptable; Schumpeter's clearing force converted into Olson's distributional stability. Bezos's own framing: productivity gains will produce "labor scarcity" — announced while Amazon lays off tens of thousands amid its automation push. Preserve both tensions honestly: (a) the augmentation defense on the record (Bajaj: faster dream-build loop, more engineers, more invention — the Habakkuk best case); (b) enclosure has never held perfectly — but note what the contest is now between: rival sovereign vaults, not a commons. Competition among enclosers does not restore the position of the person whose hands held the knowledge first.

**The legal architecture: patent versus trade secret.** Opposite instruments. The patent is a bargain with the public — full disclosure for twenty years of monopoly, the leak built into the contract. The trade secret is the refusal of the bargain — no disclosure, no expiration, protection for as long as the door holds (Coke's formula, 1886, has outlasted six patent lifetimes). Model weights live entirely on the vault side: held as trade secrets, never published, never expiring, federally enforceable since the Defend Trade Secrets Act (2016). As noncompetes come under legal pressure, trade secret litigation is the instrument employers migrate to — the knowledge becomes the property so the person no longer needs to be the prisoner. Historically, trade secrets leaked through one channel: people (sericulture out of China; Böttger's porcelain in runaway workmen's heads; Slater memorizing Arkwright's mills). Physical AI repairs the architectural flaw: no single human along the extraction pipeline holds enough to carry it out the door. You cannot memorize a hundred-billion-parameter model. You cannot be Slater. The leak channel is not being guarded better — it is being removed from the design.

**Paternalism as enclosure.** The protective framing has a deep lineage and is the most durable form of enclosure because it recruits the encloser's conscience to its own defense (Foucault's pastoral power: the shepherd's authority grows stronger the more genuinely benevolent it is). Sequence, repeated every time a knowledge domain becomes strategically important: protective framing first, enclosure as the durable result, export control as the state's reification of the private vault into a national one. Cases: the Atomic Energy Act (1946) and the "born secret" doctrine — knowledge classified at the moment of creation; the Crypto Wars — cryptography as munition, academic suppression, until Bernstein v. DOJ; professional licensure — medicine, law, clergy monopolizing knowledge domains on protection-of-the-public grounds (Larson, Abbott: licensure is enclosure wearing a caregiver's face). Current instance: AI safety framing aligned with closed-source commercial advantage (Widder, West & Whittaker, "Open (For Business)" — both "open" and "closed" AI currently consolidate power), reified by the export-control and model-diffusion regime — epistemic mercantilism, the AEA logic applied to weights. Honest position, stated in the chapter: the safety reasons are real *and* the enclosure effect is real; the second does not wait for the first to be resolved. [Disclosure note for drafting: the analysis in this section was developed in conversation with Anthropic's model; weigh and independently verify accordingly.]

**Sources.** James Boyle, "The Second Enclosure Movement and the Construction of the Public Domain" (2003) — load-bearing. Michael Polanyi, *The Tacit Dimension*. Harry Braverman, *Labor and Monopoly Capital* (1974). Andrew Ure, *The Philosophy of Manufactures* (1835). F.W. Taylor, *The Principles of Scientific Management*. Magali Sarfatti Larson, *The Rise of Professionalism*; Andrew Abbott, *The System of Professions*. Foucault, "Omnes et Singulatim" / *Security, Territory, Population* (pastoral power). Widder, West & Whittaker (2023). Defend Trade Secrets Act (2016). Atomic Energy Act (1946), Restricted Data doctrine. Bernstein v. U.S. Dept. of Justice. Prometheus reporting: CNBC, Axios, TechCrunch, Bloomberg, GeekWire (June 2026) — pull for final citations.

